%I A401740 #8 Nov 10 2026 15:13:39
%S A401740 5,13,17,37,109,113,157,197,229,233,257,293,313,349,397,421,449,457,
%T A401740 521,557,569,617,653,701,769,797,809,853,857,997,1009,1033,1181,1193,
%U A401740 1213,1289,1321,1429,1597,1637,1657,1721,1753,1777,1877,2029,2081,2089,2129
%N A401740 Pythagorean primes p for which the semiperimeter s and inradius r of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse p give another Pythagorean prime q = s^2 + r^2.
%C A401740 For each term p, there are unique integers x > y > 0 such that p = x^2 + y^2. The primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse p has odd leg u = x^2 - y^2 and even leg v = 2*x*y. Its semiperimeter is s = x*(x+y), its inradius is r = y*(x-y), and q = s^2 + r^2 = u^2 + u*v + v^2 = p^2 + u*v.
%C A401740 The value q is the squared radius of the triangle's Conway circle. A088544 records the same quantity for all primitive Pythagorean triangles, in a different order; see A401741.
%C A401740 The terms are the Pythagorean primes A002144(k) for which A401741(k) is prime, listed in increasing order of k. The corresponding primes q are congruent to 1 modulo 12 (A068228), and hence are also Pythagorean primes.
%C A401740 For each such p, Q = p*q is a product of two distinct primes. The construction in A401742 gives a 3 X 3 magic square with central entry Q^2 and nine distinct positive integer entries, at least six of them squares.
%H A401740 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401740/b401740.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..559</a>
%H A401740 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401742/a401742.txt">Semiperimeter and inradius of Pythagorean triangles and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401740 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/a400365.txt">Fourth powers of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401740 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401004/a401004.txt">Pythagorean primes, a Pell-type recurrence and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401740 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401446/a401446.txt">A Fibonacci recurrence step on Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A401740 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ConwayCircle.html">Conway Circle</a>.
%F A401740 Primes p = x^2 + y^2 for which q = x^2*(x+y)^2 + y^2*(x-y)^2 = x^4 + 2*x^3*y + 2*x^2*y^2 - 2*x*y^3 + y^4 is prime, where x and y are integers with x > y > 0.
%F A401740 a(n) = A002144(k(n)), where k(n) is the n-th index k for which A401741(k) is prime.
%e A401740 For p = 5 = 2^2 + 1^2, the legs are 3 and 4, the semiperimeter is 6 and the inradius is 1. Thus q = 6^2 + 1^2 = 37 is prime and a(1) = 5.
%e A401740 The next two Pythagorean primes, 13 and 17, give q = 15^2 + 2^2 = 229 and q = 20^2 + 3^2 = 409, both prime. Hence a(2) = 13 and a(3) = 17.
%e A401740 For p = 29, the legs are 21 and 20, and q = 35^2 + 6^2 = 1261 = 13*97, so 29 is omitted. The next Pythagorean prime, 37, gives q = 42^2 + 5^2 = 1789, which is prime. Thus a(4) = 37.
%o A401740 (Python)
%o A401740 from math import isqrt
%o A401740 from sympy import isprime, primerange
%o A401740 def a401740(limit=2500):
%o A401740     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A401740     out = []
%o A401740     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A401740         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A401740             continue
%o A401740         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A401740             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A401740             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A401740                 s, r = x*(x+y), y*(x-y)
%o A401740                 q = s*s + r*r
%o A401740                 if isprime(q):
%o A401740                     out.append(p)
%o A401740                 break
%o A401740     return out
%Y A401740 Subsequence of A002144.
%Y A401740 Cf. A002365, A002366, A068228, A088544, A400363, A400364, A400365, A401002, A401003, A401004, A401444, A401445, A401446, A401741, A401742.
